  <title>1.4.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00117</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.4.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8df3b423-e2e2-44f0-bde4-2d635006ea57</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>

      <exercise id="_14dbcfa0-6fce-471d-b730-09851a44d955" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para> Is \(y = \frac{4}{3}\) a solution of \(9y + 2 = 6y + 3\)?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Work smarter, not harder! Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is No.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_97304cdc-436a-4375-81ad-81c65c413a95" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para> Is \(y = \frac{7}{5}\) a solution of \(5y + 3 = 10y - 4\)?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is Yes.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="a747c5fd-0e3d-455c-abe5-6bde161905d2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para> Is \(y = \frac{5}{3}\) a solution of \(6y + 10 = 12y\)?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Work smarter, not harder! Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is Yes.   You seem to be stuck.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_3a00f8ce-d359-44d8-837b-1e3a937ded6e" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para> Is \(u = -\frac{1}{2}\) a solution of \(8u - 1 = 6u\)?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is No. You seem to be stuck.Who can you ask for help?</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="b3c93ee1-8ba1-43d0-be55-003676986945" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para> Tickets to the museum cost $4.50 for children and $9.00 for adults. The equation \(63 = 4.5c + 9a\) represents
          the total cost for \(c\) children’s tickets and \(a\) adult tickets. How many children’s tickets were
          sold if 4 adult tickets were sold?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is 6.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

<para>For questions 6 and 7, use the following scenario: T-shirts are on sale for $12.50. Isla has a coupon for $10.00 off.</para>
      <exercise id="_79fbb396-0500-4dfd-9527-88a0842b3ef1" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Choose the equation that represents the total cost, c, if she buys n t-shirts.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(c = 12.5n-10\)</para><para>\(c = 10n-12.5\)</para><para>\(c = 12.5(n-10)\)</para><para>\(c = 12.5(10n)\)</para></problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is c = 12.5n-10.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="_3cc310a3-9e72-4995-907d-60606fc94d33" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>How many T-shirts can she buy if she spends all of her $40?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is 4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="_9eca9bca-6517-4bb7-b15a-6bc5daf1e385" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>An artist is selling children's crafts. Necklaces cost $2.25 each, and bracelets cost $1.50 per each. Select
          <emphasis>three</emphasis> combinations of necklaces and bracelets that the artist could sell for exactly $12.00</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is: 2 necklaces and 5 bracelets, 4 necklaces and 2 bracelets, No necklaces and 8 bracelets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="_55d7874c-c528-49c9-a123-a73eb0d5dd97" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Volunteer drivers are needed to bring 80 students to the championship baseball game. Drivers either have cars,
          which can seat 4 students, or vans, which can seat 6 students. The equation \(4c+6v=80\) describes the
          relationship between the number of cars, \(c\), and number of vans, \(v\), that can transport exactly 80 students.
          Select <emphasis>four</emphasis> statements that are true about the situation.</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is: c=14 and v=4 is a pair of solutions to the equation. If 6 cars go and 11 vans go, there will be extra space. If 20 cars go, no vans are needed. 8 vans and 8 cars are numbers that meet the constraints in this situation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

<para>Use the following scenario for questions 11 and 12:</para>
<para>The drama club is printing T-shirts for its members. The printing company charges a certain amount for each T-shirt plus a setup fee of $40. There are 21 students in the drama club.</para>
      <exercise id="d460c6a6-e642-4be8-b480-b5e6c6985408" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
        <para>If there are 21 students in the club and the T-shirt order costs a total of $187, how much does each T-shirt
          cost?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is 7; \(40+21c=187\rightarrow c=7.\) Each t-shirt costs $7.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="_9fddb6dc-98b6-4911-ba3a-d1ee7c8ebe24" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>The equation \(201.50=f+6.50(21)\) represents the cost of printing the T-shirts at a second printing company.
          Find the solution to the equation.</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is 65; The cost of printing the t-shirts is $65.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
  </section>
